<h1>Getting Started</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Start with advisory matching, then add heuristic analysis only where its bounded evidence helps.</p>

<h2>Requirements and versions</h2>
{% if versions.vexes.version == versions.vexes.sourceVersion %}
<p>Node.js {{ versions.vexes.node }} or later is required. The published npm package, GitHub source tree, and this wiki currently describe v{{ versions.vexes.version }}.</p>
{% else %}
<p>Node.js {{ versions.vexes.node }} or later is required. The published npm package is currently v{{ versions.vexes.version }}. The GitHub source tree and this wiki describe v{{ versions.vexes.sourceVersion }} until that source is published.</p>
{% endif %}

<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Published package</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npx @penumbraforge/vexes scan</span>

<span class="cmt"># Current source</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">git clone https://github.com/penumbraforge/vexes.git</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">node vexes/bin/vexes.js scan --path /path/to/project</span></div>

<h2>First scan</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes scan --path ./project</span></div>
<p><code>scan</code> reads recognized dependency inputs and sends package names, versions, and ecosystem identifiers to OSV. It reports versions that OSV places in affected ranges. This is deterministic matching to an upstream advisory record, not proof of reachability, exploitation, or malicious intent.</p>

<h2>Add heuristic evidence</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes analyze</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes analyze --deep</span></div>
<p><code>analyze</code> currently covers npm/PyPI registry evidence. <code>--deep</code> downloads and inspects a bounded set of selected entry/install-like files: JavaScript with vendored Acorn and Python with pattern matching. It is not a whole-package review. When sampling occurs, the result remains explicitly incomplete and exits 2 while retaining findings.</p>

<h2>Interpret the result</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Exit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Requested supported checks completed with no finding at the active threshold. Not a safety verdict.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>One or more findings met the active reporting/failure policy.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Error or incomplete requested evidence. Read the warnings; do not treat it as clean.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Data routing</h2>
<ul>
  <li>OSV scans send dependency coordinates to <code>api.osv.dev</code>.</li>
  <li>Analysis and licenses may contact npm, PyPI, provenance, and deps.dev endpoints.</li>
  <li>Deep analysis downloads bounded package archives.</li>
  <li>AI explanation is opt-in and sends sanitized finding data to the configured local or hosted model endpoint.</li>
</ul>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Use it as a review aid.</strong> A CRITICAL or HIGH label prioritizes evidence; it does not itself say a package is malicious. A clean-looking result can still miss unpublished advisories, unsupported inputs, unsampled code, runtime-only behavior, or a novel technique.</div>
